From 2ea905872067f30efc29a3b3fa7438ba110684d1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 21:32:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] nvkms, nvidia-drm: HDR10+ next to the HDR InfoFrame, written
 with each flip

HDR10+ needs its vendor-specific InfoFrame (IEEE OUI 90-84-8B), with the
dynamic metadata of each frame, next to the HDR InfoFrame. NVKMS could not
send both on Turing, Ampere and Ada: their HAL sends the HDR InfoFrame
through the vendor-specific slot of the SOR (nvEvo1SendHdmiInfoFrame()), so
a flip with both is refused (ValidateHdmiVsif()) and the HDR InfoFrame is
skipped while a client VSIF is set (SendHDRInfoFrame()). On DisplayPort no
vendor InfoFrame goes out at all. And KAPI makes every change of the VSIF a
mode set where InfoFrames are not flip-synchronized (all GPUs but T239,
Blackwell included), which shuts the head down with FRL timings.

The hardware has room for it. The SOR has a generic InfoFrame slot next to
the vendor-specific one on these GPUs (NV9171_SF_HDMI_INFO_IDX_GENERIC_INFOFRAME,
31 bytes), which NVKMS uses only for the EMP of HDMI VRR; AD10x has six
shared generic slots per head (NVC771_SF_SHARED_GENERIC_*), of which NVKMS
uses only the first, for the Adaptive-Sync SDP on DisplayPort.

A new connector property of nvidia-drm, HDR10PLUS_VSIF, takes the 27 bytes
of payload of the HDR10+ InfoFrame of a frame (OUI first). It goes to NVKMS
as the client's VSIF metadata, and:
- on HDMI, without generic shared InfoFrames (Turing, Ampere, Ada), NVKMS
  writes it into the generic InfoFrame slot, every frame; the HDR InfoFrame
  stays where it was, byte for byte, and so does what the vendor-specific
  slot carries without a client VSIF. On GA10x, where RM keeps that slot
  for its ACR workaround (NV0073_CTRL_SYSTEM_CAPS_HDMI21_SW_ACR_BUG_3275257),
  it is not offered. HDMI VRR sends its EMP in that slot every frame, and
  NVKMS turns HDMI VRR on in each mode set of a display that declares it,
  from 50 Hz and 720 lines up (KAPI allows Adaptive-Sync in all of them),
  whether flips use VRR or not: AD10x then sends the HDR10+ InfoFrame in
  shared generic slot 2, laid out as NVKMS lays out an HDMI InfoFrame in
  these slots on T239 and Blackwell; Turing and Ampere refuse it;
- on HDMI with generic shared InfoFrames (T239, Blackwell) it goes through
  the vendor-specific slot, which is apart from the HDR one there;
- on DisplayPort it goes out as an InfoFrame SDP (version 1.3) built as
  NVKMS builds the one of the HDR InfoFrame, in SHARED_GENERIC2 of AD10x or
  SHARED_GENERIC4 with generic shared InfoFrames. On AD10x the slot sends it
  without the checksum of the hardware, the checksum of an HDMI InfoFrame: a
  shared generic slot that holds one keeps it in DB0, right after the
  header, and in an InfoFrame SDP DB0 is the version of the InfoFrame (an
  InfoFrame SDP has no checksum). NVKMS writes its own SDPs into these
  slots without it on T239 and Blackwell; whether AD10x would write it on a
  DisplayPort SOR is not documented. Turing and Ampere have two generic
  SDPs, both in use (VSC, HDR): not offered there;
- a change of it alone is carried by a flip (hdmiVsifMetadataDynamic of
  KAPI), and the flip writes only that InfoFrame (nvHadesUpdateHdr10Plus()),
  not every InfoFrame as nvUpdateInfoFrames() does. Its validation refuses
  what cannot go out, so a client learns it from the commit.
nvidia-drm attaches the property only to connectors through which NVKMS says
it can go out (NV_KMS_DPY_ATTRIBUTE_HADES_HDR10PLUS, read-only). It cannot
be set with NV_HDMI_VSIF_METADATA, nor in an HDMI 3D frame-packed mode.

Each head keeps the slot its HDR10+ InfoFrame goes out in, and turns off
the one before when it moves, but never the generic slot while the EMP of
HDMI VRR holds it. A refusal says why in the kernel log, once for each GPU,
head and reason in a row ("Hades: HDR10+ refused on head N: ...").

The decisions and the bytes of the packets are in nvkms-hades.h, which a
host test runs with NVIDIA's packet writers of AD10x. The write is not
synchronized with the flip: InfoFrames are flip-synchronized only on T239.
Written in the same flip ioctl, before its update, a packet goes out in the
vertical blanking where that flip takes effect; whether a sink takes the
metadata of a frame from it is for the sink to show.
